A||||6||Motorhead||Engine/Trans Dolly
Z||000000||Motorhead||06-08-99||02:55 PM||||I'm presently building a dolly for removing my Engine/Trans cradle out the bottom of the car. My biggest concern is car and ground clearances. <p>The Haynes' manual mentions supporting the cradle at or above the normal driveline level to prevent having to remove the front tires to get the car low enough to support the cradle,however, this would require having to lift the rear higher to extract the engine from under the car.  I fgure the lower I make my dolly, the less I have to jack the car.<p>I'm also making a "jacking beam" to allow the use of a single jack to lift the car at it's rear jack points at the centerline of the car.<p>If anybody has built such a dolly I'd welcome any comments, especially regarding clearance heights for getting the engine out from under the car!<p>
Z||000001||PaUL||06-08-99||06:19 PM||||hey Motorhead, I made a dolly out of wood. I used 2 inch diameter rubber wheels screwed into the bottom to provide just the right height. I got them from Ace Hardware. The dolly was made with just 4 pieces of wood arranged in a sort of V shape to match the bottom of the cradle. <p>I used it only once, I prefering to lift the engine out with a hoist. My moms been using it to move stuff around ever since.    
Z||000002||RFMike||06-08-99||07:00 PM||||Paul,<br>You're either scaring me or amazing; I'm not sure which.  Tell us more.
Z||000003||PaUL||06-08-99||08:30 PM||||LOL, scary and amazing? The dolly I built was real strong, used 2 2x8 pieces of wood with 2 2x4 crossmembers. The wheels looked heavy duty, but I admit that when all the weight was on them, they deformed and it was kind of hard to wheel it around. 
Z||000004||Motorhead||06-09-99||03:26 AM||||I plan on using this thing again for removing the engine from a wreck I'm trying to get ahold of, and a second Fiero, so I'm welding it up using 3/8 inch formed steel channel.<p>The height of the supports for the cradle will be about 6 inches.  I'm just concerned about getting the rear of the car high enough to roll the engine out from under the car.
Z||000005||PaUL||06-09-99||10:28 AM||||Motorhead, sounds like a good plan. Just be sure to use wheels or casters that can take the weight. Go with the biggest diameter wheels you can use and still maintain the desired height.
Z||000006||Motorhead||06-09-99||05:32 PM||||Finished the dolly today. Height came out at 8 inches because of larger wheels.<p>Wheels are steel, with steel ball bearing casters, don't think I'll have any trouble with them handling the weight.<p>Gonna' pull the engine and trans this weekend.
